nding region of the unknown  which can be critical for seeking out  family members and for submitting case  reports with dna profile results  sg migrants as i said are found in  criminal databases  so as i've already said marginalization  begets marginalization  through that concept that forced  migrants do not disappear but they are  forcibly disappeared  these structural factors prevent knowing  about the unknown  in the absence of family data accessing  information  on origins by other means is therefore  critical  so we have to figure out where to send  this information  or who to start asking to go ask  around for family members but we can't  do that if we don't know  the geographic location where this  unknown individual originated  so then how can we improve case  identification success  well motivated by these increasing  number of unsuc successful  identifications of missing migrants  how can we address this barrier to  improve rates of positive identification  and repatriation of the border debt so  my response to this question is to  engage try hybrid ancestry estimates  so the uh proportions of  ancestry for each individual the  case recovery location so the gps  coordinates where that individual was  recovered along the border  and because these the cases that i'm  working on to build these  this analysis or model is are already  positively identified  then i have their place of origin or  their sending region already  and i'm using these three sets of  variables to develop a  biogeographic profile which is a  model that allows me to predict sending  region  to aid in the skin matching and to  enable  repatriation  and how is this possible so  genetic diversity in latin america is  very high  studies using genetic and skeletal data  alike have revealed differences in  ancestry  for many of the geospatially defined  groups that are distributed across latin  america  so we can use this spatial organization  or structure  in ancestry estimates to reveal  information on their spending regions  we can also capitalize upon information  that we can get from those who are  successful  at crossing the border so we can  understand the habits of the living  to shed greater light on the context of  the dead  so demographic research and migration  networks have found distinctive  transit pathways whereby migrants of  different origins  so with different state or city of birth  or residents are associated with  particular destinations  so you come from a particular place you  are more likely to cross the border  at a particular location the pathways  through mexico and the crossing points  along the u.s mexico border are  shared by experience so if  you have family who have successfully  crossed or friends they transmit that  information back  to your hometown that information then  informs the next um persons who  attempt the journey so it's a  perpetualization of these  same transit pathways that are clearly  linked by  point of origin and point of destination  and we can use these trends then to  predict the sending region  for the missing migrants  how did i develop this procedure  so i started out with 150 cases from the  pima county office of the medical  examiner in arizona  i applied model-based clustering  geospatial  analysis machine learning and  traditional statistical methods  to produce this biogeographic profile  and i'll explain how i've done that in  more detail  as we move along to this goal then the  relationships among  ancestry composition so that trihybrid  ancestry  breakdown the geographic location of the  remains  so those gps coordinates on the border  the sending region of the place of  origin of the individual  their sex and their case reporting date  were all investigated  and i took that information then built  models that predict the individual's  documented sending region as a micro  regional category  on the one hand and then remarkably it's  even possible to predict the gis  coordinates for that sending region when  those coordinates are taken as the  centroid  of the area  state typically from which they  originated  so how do we  calculate craniometric measures of  ancestry  um so i'm sure you're familiar with  ancestry estimates being derived from  genetic data  here what i've done is i've rewritten  the mathematics for  the genetic which is categorical  trait analysis to a continuous trait  analysis  so that i produce very similar  results to the genetic data  using almost identical or highly  comparable  mathematical procedures how is this  possible through craniometrics  well measurements of the cranium or  craniometrics have been used as proxies  for genetic data  to study microevolutionary processes  trace patterns of variation  and reveal information on population  history and individual biology  all through anthropology for its history  and this is because craniometrics have  low plasticity with high to moderate  heritabilities  proportions of ancestry then can be  calculated from cranium metrics  using model based clustering  implemented with the methods of finite  mixture analysis  and what's exciting about this approach  is that it's an admixture approach  that's comparable to the methods that  are used in genetics so if you're  familiar with the software admixture or  structure  it's the same kind of philosophy and  it's preferable  i would argue to the traditional single  group hard classification methods that  are used  um ubiquitously in forensic anthropology  because they don't  allow us to get at the nuance of the  geography so by producing these  proportions  we can identify signatures that  represent  geographic regions these geographic  regions need not be  labeled in the same way as a  geopolitical region so we can actually  map them onto real geographic space  and see how they vary and then create  our own  partitions or organization to those that  make  sense based on the  relationships that we're seeing in the  ancestry estimates and in the gis  coordinates  so how is how are these ancestry  estimates actually produced  each individual's ancestry is reframed  as a mixture of parental contributors  and if it's tri-hybrid we presume that  latin american peoples are  have their ancestry made up by european  african and indigenous components  so we calculate the mixture of those  components for each individual  or we can think of them as the degree of  affinity  between groups as well so once you have  those three  components then you can look at how much  sharing  occurs between different populations  based on the similarity in their  ancestry signatures  and this differs fundamentally from a  single ancestry classification for  multiple reasons first that we start out  with the unknowns  individual and we have a reference  sample of individuals as well  but we strip all of those individuals  from  there we strip away from those  individuals their identifiers  so no sex no population no information  on them whatsoever  we just use their data we subject it to  the model based clustering and we get a  probabilistic  allocation so we can see how  individuals share between all of the  clusters  and each one of those clusters adds up  to one  so sorry of the combination of the  clusters add up to one  each one of the clusters contributes in  some proportion  of that one or one hundred percent  so individuals vary in each of those  um three ancestry proportions which then  serves as providing a signature  for the individual and then when  aggregated in terms of a population  or a region or however you want to  partition it you get a mean signature  that then allows you for tests of  differences etcetera  a single ancestry classification starts  out with  predefined groups so presumably  geographically defined populations for  which you have information  you have your unknown individual and  your unknown individual is allocated  into one of those groups even though  that individual may not  actually 